        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Point of Use Water Heater Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            A point of use (POU) water heater is any small heater installed at or near the fixture it serves, usually under a sink or inside a cabinet within a few feet of the tap. Instead of pushing hot water through 40 or 60 feet of pipe from a basement tank, it heats the water where it is used. The category covers two quite different machines: compact storage tanks, usually 2.5 to 7 gallons, and small electric tankless units that heat only while water flows. Both are almost always electric, because running a gas line and a flue to a vanity cabinet makes no sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem they solve is distance. Hot water sitting in a pipe cools between uses, so every time you open a faraway tap you first drain a pipe full of cold water. A half-inch copper line holds roughly 0.01 gallons per foot, so a 60-foot run holds more than half a gallon before any heat arrives, and more once you allow for the water spent warming the pipe itself. At a bathroom faucet limited to 1.2 or 1.5 gallons per minute that is a 30 to 45 second wait, and behind a 0.5 GPM low-flow aerator it can stretch past a minute.
          </p>

          <p>
            A POU heater sidesteps that wait because the hot water starts a foot or two from the spout. It also fills gaps the main system never covered: a detached garage, a basement bar, a workshop sink, a pool house, or an addition where extending the hot line back to the main heater would mean opening finished walls and ceilings. In those spots it is often the cheapest way to get any hot water at all, and the only way that does not involve a contractor tearing out drywall.
          </p>

          <p>
            What it is not is a substitute for a whole-house heater. A 120-volt POU unit makes a modest amount of heat, and even the larger 240-volt models run out of capacity at shower flow rates once incoming water gets cold in winter. The physics are covered in the sizing section below, but the short version is that heating a lot of water quickly takes a lot of power, and a single under-sink heater does not have it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still mapping out the options for your house as a whole, our overview of the <a href="/types-of-water-heaters/">types of water heaters</a> puts POU units in context alongside conventional tanks, whole-house tankless, heat pump and solar systems. This guide stays focused on the small end: what POU heaters do well, what they cost, and how to tell whether one belongs under your sink.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mini-tank-vs-tankless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Mini-Tank vs Point of Use Tankless
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mini-tank heaters look like a shrunken version of a standard electric water heater: an insulated steel tank, usually glass-lined, with a single heating element and a thermostat. The common sizes are 2.5, 4 and 7 gallons, and most plug into a standard 120-volt outlet and draw around 12 to 13 amps, roughly 1,400 to 1,500 watts. Bosch's Tronic 3000 T line and the mini tanks sold by Rheem and A.O. Smith are the familiar examples. Because they store hot water, they deliver full temperature the moment you open the tap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Their limit is volume. A 2.5-gallon tank set at 120°F, blended with cold water at the faucet, gives you somewhat more than its rated capacity in comfortably warm water before it starts to fade. That is plenty for hand washing and rinsing a few dishes, and nowhere near enough for a shower. Recovery is slow, because a 1,500-watt element only moves about 5,100 BTU per hour. Reheating a full 2.5 gallons through a 70°F rise takes somewhere around 17 to 20 minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Point of use tankless heaters have no storage at all. Water passes over a bare element or through a heated chamber only while it flows, so they never run out, but they can only add as much heat as their wattage allows at that instant. 120-volt models are typically rated around 2.5 to 3.5 kilowatts, while 240-volt POU units run from roughly 3.5 kW up to around 13 kW, where they start to overlap with small whole-house units. Stiebel Eltron, EcoSmart and Rheem all sell POU tankless lines, and our <a href="/best-electric-tankless-water-heater/">electric tankless buyer's guide</a> covers both the whole-house and point-of-use ends of that range.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical difference comes down to how the fixture is used. A mini-tank is better when flow is high but brief and the available circuit is small, because the stored water carries the burst and the element catches up afterward. A tankless POU unit is better when use is frequent and you never want the supply to run dry, as long as the flow stays low or the incoming water is warm. Tankless units also need a minimum flow to switch on, often somewhere around 0.2 to 0.5 GPM depending on the model, which matters behind very low-flow aerators.
          </p>

