            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Neither type is the better product in the abstract. The right one is whichever fits the fixture's flow, your winter water temperature and the electrical circuit you can realistically get to that cabinet. Those three numbers are worth writing down before you shop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-it-fits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Where a Point of Use Heater Earns Its Place
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The far bathroom or kitchen sink</h3>

          <p>
            The classic case is a primary bathroom or kitchen at the opposite end of the house from the water heater. A mini-tank under the vanity gives instant hot water for hand washing and shaving, while showers and the tub still draw from the main heater as before. This works best at lavatory sinks, where each use is short and frequent and the wait is felt every single time. At a kitchen sink it works too, though heavier dish rinsing can drain a 2.5-gallon tank, so a 4-gallon model is the safer size there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garages, workshops and outbuildings</h3>

          <p>
            Detached garages, workshops, pool houses and basement bars are the next best fit. Running a new hot line from the main heater might mean trenching across a yard or opening finished ceilings, while a single dedicated circuit is usually far simpler. For a garage utility sink, a 4- or 7-gallon mini-tank covers cleanup comfortably. For a small accessory dwelling or in-law suite with its own shower, you are past point of use and into a properly sized central heater, and that is where our <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">tankless water heater picks</a> come in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A booster on a long run</h3>

          <p>
            A mini-tank can also be plumbed in series as a booster: the hot line from the main heater feeds the mini-tank's inlet, and its outlet feeds the faucet. The tank delivers hot water instantly, and by the time it has been drawn down, hot water from the main heater has arrived to refill it. Because the incoming water is already warm, the small element barely has to work. The same arrangement is a known fix for the brief burst of cold water some whole-house tankless systems send between back-to-back uses.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">After a relocation or a remodel</h3>

          <p>
            Moving the main heater can make one fixture's wait noticeably worse. Swapping a basement tank for a wall-hung tankless on an exterior wall, chosen because the venting is shorter there, can lengthen the run to a far bathroom by twenty feet or more. A POU unit at that one bathroom is often cheaper than adding a recirculation loop. If you are planning that kind of swap, our guide to <a href="/replace-water-heater-with-tankless/">replacing a water heater with tankless</a> covers the relocation questions before you commit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: Flow, Temperature Rise and Your Circuit
          </h2>

          <p>
            POU sizing comes down to the same two numbers that decide whole-house tankless sizing: flow and temperature rise. Flow is what the fixture uses, set mostly by its aerator or showerhead. Temperature rise is the gap between your incoming water and the temperature you want at the tap. Our <a href="/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/">tankless sizing guide</a> walks through the full whole-house method; the version below is the short form for a single fixture.
          </p>

          <p>
            The useful rule of thumb for electric heating is that one kilowatt raises one gallon per minute by about 6.8°F. So a 3.5 kW unit gives roughly a 24°F rise at 1 GPM, or about 48°F at 0.5 GPM. With 50°F groundwater, which is typical of a northern winter, and a target of 105°F at the faucet, you need a 55°F rise. That same 3.5 kW unit only gets there at about 0.45 GPM. It works behind a 0.5 GPM aerator and falls well short on an ordinary 1.5 GPM kitchen faucet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scale the numbers up and the limits become obvious. A shower at 2.0 GPM needing that same 55°F rise calls for about 16 kW, which is beyond any POU unit and squarely in whole-house electric tankless territory. In the Gulf states, where incoming water can sit near 70°F, the rise drops to around 35°F and the same shower needs about 10 kW. That is why electric POU showers are realistic in Florida and marginal at best in Minnesota.
          </p>

