                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.0 GPM</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">55°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 16 kW</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For mini-tanks the question is volume rather than wattage. Estimate how much hot water a single use draws: a hand wash takes well under half a gallon, and rinsing a sink of dishes might take two or three. A 2.5-gallon tank covers a bathroom sink, a 4-gallon tank suits most kitchens, and 6 or 7 gallons suits a utility or bar sink that sees heavier use in bursts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then check the electrical side, because that is usually what decides the model. Plug-in mini-tanks want a 15 or 20 amp outlet that is not shared with a garbage disposal or dishwasher. POU tankless units are generally hardwired on a dedicated circuit sized to the manufacturer's label, and the breaker and wire must match it: as a general guide, 20 amps on 12-gauge copper, 30 amps on 10-gauge and 40 amps on 8-gauge. If the panel has no spare spaces or no spare capacity, that changes the cost picture far more than the price of the heater does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Installation and the Code Points People Miss
          </h2>

          <p>
            A plug-in mini-tank is one of the few water heaters a competent DIYer can install, with some caveats. It connects with braided supply lines between the hot angle stop and the faucet's hot inlet, sits on a shelf or wall bracket inside the cabinet, and plugs into an outlet. Manufacturers' instructions generally require filling the tank completely and bleeding the air out at the faucet before power goes on, because energizing an empty tank can burn out the element almost immediately.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A tank of any size needs its relief valve piped.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every storage water heater, including a 2.5-gallon under-sink model, has a temperature and pressure relief valve. Plumbing codes generally require its discharge to run to a safe place such as a drain or indirect waste receptor, not to dangle into the vanity cabinet. Check your local code before you close the doors on it.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            That relief valve is the code point most often skipped on DIY installs. Many jurisdictions also want a drain pan under a tank installed above a finished floor, which is exactly where under-sink tanks live. Thermal expansion is the other overlooked issue: on a closed system with a check valve or pressure-reducing valve, heated water has nowhere to expand, and a small expansion tank or another relief path keeps the T&P valve from weeping into the cabinet.
          </p>

          <p>
            POU tankless units are simpler on the plumbing side, since there is no tank to drain, and harder on the electrical side. They are hardwired to a dedicated circuit, and in most places that is permitted work for a licensed electrician rather than a homeowner project. Some models ship with or call for a pressure relief device; follow the installation manual rather than assuming. Mount the unit with service clearance and where you can reach the inlet filter screen, which clogs on hard water and quietly cuts flow until the heater stops firing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both types need an accessible shutoff and both deserve occasional maintenance. Many mini-tanks have an anode rod and benefit from being drained now and then to clear sediment, while POU tankless units build scale on their elements just like their larger cousins. The descaling approach in our <a href="/how-to-flush-a-tankless-water-heater/">tankless flushing guide</a> applies on a smaller scale, and hard water shortens the interval considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, set the temperature sensibly. A 120°F setpoint is the usual recommendation for limiting scald risk, and many under-sink tanks ship with a dial that goes considerably hotter. If young children or older adults use that sink, keep the setpoint low or add a thermostatic mixing valve at the outlet. It is a cheap part that removes most of the scald risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What a Point of Use Heater Costs to Buy and Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Unit prices are modest. Mini-tanks typically sell somewhere in the low hundreds of dollars depending on size and brand, and 120-volt POU tankless units can cost less than that, while 240-volt models run into the several hundreds. Treat those as rough retail ranges rather than quotes, since prices move and sales are frequent; check current listings before you budget.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation is where the real spread lives. A plug-in mini-tank under a sink with an existing outlet can be a one-hour DIY job or a short plumber's visit. Adding a new dedicated circuit commonly costs a few hundred dollars, and more if the run from the panel is long or the panel is full. A hardwired 240-volt POU unit nearly always involves an electrician, and if the service needs an upgrade the project can cost many times the heater itself. Our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/">tankless water heater cost breakdown</a> covers panel and wiring costs in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running cost is small because the volumes are small. Heating half a gallon of water through a 55°F rise takes about 230 BTU, or roughly 0.07 kilowatt-hours. At an electricity price around 17 cents per kWh, that is about a cent per hand wash. Even heavy daily use at a single sink rarely adds more than a few dollars a month to the bill, and often less.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mini-tanks do carry a standby loss: the tank sheds heat around the clock and the element tops it back up. On a 2.5-gallon tank that loss is small in absolute terms, because the surface area is tiny, but it is not zero. A plug-in timer, or simply switching off the heater in a guest bath that sits unused for weeks, trims it. POU tankless units have no standby loss at all, which is their main efficiency advantage over a mini-tank.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Math at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6.8°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rise per kW at 1 GPM</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~0.07 kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Energy per half-gallon hand wash</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~16 kW</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Needed for a 2 GPM winter shower</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">17-20 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mini-tank reheat, 2.5 gal at 1.5 kW</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            There is also a hidden saving in water. Every 30-second wait at a far tap pours a little cold water down the drain, several times a day, every day. A POU heater ends that waste at the sink it serves, which matters most if you are on a well with a slow-recovering pressure tank or you pay high combined water and sewer rates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When a Point of Use Heater Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            POU heaters are the wrong answer as a whole-house solution. People sometimes plan to replace a main tank with a handful of under-sink units plus one in the shower. In a cold climate the shower unit alone needs a whole-house-class circuit, and every extra unit multiplies wiring, maintenance and the number of things that can fail. One properly sized central heater, tank or tankless, is usually cheaper and better. If you are weighing that route, start with our <a href="/what-is-a-tankless-water-heater/">explainer on what a tankless water heater is</a> and how it differs from a tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            They are also wrong when the real complaint is the wait at several fixtures. If every bathroom takes a minute to run hot, a recirculation system fixes all of them at once. That can be a dedicated return loop, or a pump at the heater paired with an under-sink crossover valve at the farthest fixture, and some whole-house tankless models have recirculation pumps built in. Recirculation spends energy keeping pipes warm, so a timer, a motion sensor or an on-demand button keeps it efficient.
          </p>

          <p>
            Instant hot water dispensers are a separate product altogether. If what you want is near-boiling water for tea or instant noodles, an under-sink dispenser with its own small tap is the right tool. A POU heater feeds the regular faucet at normal washing temperatures, and running it hot enough for tea would be a scald hazard at the main tap.
          </p>

          <p>
            And they are wrong where the electrical panel cannot support them. If the only way to install a 240-volt POU tankless unit is a service upgrade, a mini-tank on an existing 120-volt circuit, or a recirculation pump, will almost always deliver the same result for far less money. The heater is the cheap part of this project; the wire behind it often is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within those limits, a point of use water heater is one of the cheapest comfort upgrades in a house. Choose the type by the fixture's flow, your winter water temperature and the circuit you can reach, pipe the relief valve properly, and it will quietly fix the one tap that has annoyed you for years. Shoppers comparing makers across the category can see who builds what in our guide to <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 120V tankless for a kitchen faucet in a cold climate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At 1.5 GPM and a 55°F rise you need around 12 kW, and a 3.5 kW unit turns that into lukewarm water. Run the rise math against your winter groundwater temperature before you buy, fit a lower-flow aerator, or choose a mini-tank that stores hot water instead of heating it on the fly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the relief valve discharge unpiped</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every storage tank has a temperature and pressure relief valve, and codes generally want its discharge piped to a drain or safe location. A T&P valve that dumps scalding water into a vanity cabinet is a safety problem and a failed inspection. Pipe it properly and add a pan where your code calls for one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Powering a tank before it is full</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging in a mini-tank before it is filled and purged of air can burn out the element almost immediately, and that failure is rarely covered by warranty. Open the faucet, let water run steadily with no sputtering, and only then plug it in or switch on the breaker.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sharing a circuit with the disposal or dishwasher</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mini-tank draws around 12 amps on its own. Put it on the same circuit as a garbage disposal or dishwasher and the breaker trips whenever both run. Give it its own outlet on a circuit that is not already carrying a large kitchen load, or plan for a new circuit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a point of use unit to run a shower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2.0 GPM winter shower needs roughly 16 kW of electric heat, which is whole-house territory. No under-sink tank or small tankless unit will keep up. If a far shower needs help, size a proper central or zone heater, or fix the wait with recirculation instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a point of use water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a small water heater installed at or near the fixture it serves, usually under a sink. It comes in two forms: mini storage tanks of roughly 2.5 to 7 gallons, and small electric tankless units that heat only while water flows. Both eliminate the long wait for hot water at fixtures far from the main heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a point of use water heater worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it for a single sink that waits a long time for hot water, or for a garage, workshop or bar sink that has no hot line. It is not worth it as a replacement for a whole-house heater, and if several fixtures share the same long wait, a recirculation pump usually fixes them all for less.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a point of use water heater run a shower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally not in a cold climate. A 2.0 GPM shower with 50°F incoming water needs about 16 kW of electric heat, beyond any point of use unit. In warm regions with incoming water near 70°F, a larger 240-volt unit of around 10 kW or more can work. Check the label's rated flow at your temperature rise first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a point of use water heater do I need for a bathroom sink?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a mini-tank, 2.5 gallons covers a bathroom sink comfortably, because hand washing uses well under half a gallon. For a tankless unit, the answer depends on flow and winter water temperature: around 4 kW handles a 0.5 GPM aerator with 50°F incoming water, while a standard 1.2 GPM faucet would need closer to 10 kW.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do point of use water heaters need a dedicated circuit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most plug-in mini-tanks run on a standard 120-volt outlet but should not share a circuit with a disposal or dishwasher. Point of use tankless units are generally hardwired to a dedicated circuit sized to the manufacturer's label, and 240-volt models need a double-pole breaker and the matching wire gauge, usually installed by an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a point of use water heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A plug-in mini-tank is a reasonable DIY job if you can connect supply lines and you pipe the relief valve discharge correctly. Fill the tank before powering it. Hardwired tankless units and any new circuit usually require a licensed electrician and often a permit, so check your local rules before you start.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Point of use water heater vs recirculation pump: which is better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A point of use heater is better for one problem fixture or a location with no hot line at all. A recirculation pump is better when several fixtures all wait a long time, because it keeps hot water close to every tap at once. Recirculation uses energy keeping pipes warm, so pair it with a timer or on-demand control.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

